## Opening

August 2026, overnight, the north end of the DFW terminal area. A steel-and-glass box 278 feet long, 136 feet across, weighing 3,320 tons, creeps down a closed taxiway on self-propelled modular transporters. A two-week overnight window, six mega-modules — the contractor describes the largest as the largest ever moved for a terminal expansion.[^1] Survey tolerance at set-down: about three-quarters of an inch.[^1]

By the time that module makes the trip, its column grid is fixed. Its floor-to-floor height is fixed. Its MEP risers, its baggage-belt penetrations, its structural allowance for what could ever sit above it — all fixed, months earlier, in a factory. The board has never seen those decisions as a decision list.

That is the first thing wrong with the phrase "before the concrete cures." The operative deadline at Terminal F is release-for-fabrication per module. For the six Phase 1 modules moved in August 2026, it has already passed.[^2] For the FIS module, the Skylink node module, and the Phase 2 apron modules that would carry MARS provisions, it has not — but no one outside Innovation Next+ has the calendar on paper. Getting that calendar is the first act of governance the register performs.

**Thesis in three sentences.** American Airlines — Terminal F's sole tenant on a lease that now runs to 2043[^3] — has already publicly declared that Terminal F is not its premium anchor at DFW: the 37,000-square-foot Admirals Club (American's largest anywhere) goes to Terminal C, Flagship Check-In goes to Terminal D near D30, and Terminal F gets a Provisions grab-and-go.[^4] The historical record on purpose-built single-carrier hub terminals says the buildings that survived a strategy shift did so by encoding generic hub *form*, not carrier-specific finishes.[^5] The correct response is neither to override the tenant with premium bones it did not ask for, nor to accept the announced program as the whole 2043 program: it is to codify a written reversibility register — a board-adopted governance instrument, paired with a U&L side letter — that locks in generic hub geometry (MARS-capable stands, an FIS sized to a commissioned 2043 international peak, a Skylink node that survives an unscheduled single-loop morning) and preserves a small, priced list of Flagship-convertible shell options that American can trigger, on the carrier's balance sheet, when the carrier is ready.

