## The argument

### The tenant has already told you what Terminal F is

In July 2026 American Airlines rolled out its "next-generation premium experience at DFW." The announcement named a 37,000-square-foot Admirals Club in Terminal C adjacent to Skylink — described as the airline's largest club anywhere. It named a Flagship Check-In facility in Terminal D near the D30 checkpoint, serving Flagship First, Flagship Business, and eligible AAdvantage elites through a separate entrance and dedicated processing. It named, for Terminal F, a Provisions by Admirals Club grab-and-go: a scaled-down offering for transit passengers with short layovers, not a Flagship Lounge and not a full Admirals Club.[^4]

This is the party with the most information about its own 2043 network telling DFW where the premium program lives in the physical footprint. It is also the airline's revealed preference on which of DFW's five terminals is the premium anchor, and it is not the terminal DFW is now building.

The run prompt names American's premium pivot as the reason to design Terminal F to absorb it. That premise is right about the pivot and wrong about the terminal. The pivot is real — Delta's Q4 2025 premium at $5.70B crossed main cabin at $5.62B for the first time in Delta's history; American's own targets call for narrowbody premium share to move from ~25% to ~40% and international lie-flat count to grow more than 50% by decade's end; the 787-9P carries 51 Flagship Suites against 30 on the previous configuration.[^6] [^17] That is a structural yield play with physical implications for widebody gate mix, lounge seat pitch, and premium check-in envelope. But American is executing that pivot at DFW through Terminals C and D. Terminal F is being scoped as a connecting building with a grab-and-go.

Two readings of the Provisions signal are defensible and the register has to survive both. The *tactical* reading: Provisions is a live 2026 capital-allocation signal, not a settled 2043 program, and the airport should preserve the physical envelope that would let American change its mind in 2032 without a nine-figure mid-lease retrofit. The *strategic* reading: American has permanently allocated its DFW premium capital to Terminals C and D, and Terminal F is a connecting-only facility for the life of the lease. If the strategic reading is right, the shell allowance is dead weight for 17 years — foregone lease revenue on the reserved footprint at DFW's average terminal rent (~$100–150/sf/yr on comparable non-signatory space), plus displaced retail. On the shell footprints priced in Card 3 (~19,000 sf), the negative-carry order of magnitude is $1.9–2.9M per year, or $32–49M over 17 years discounted at 4% — real money, and less than the low-case at-grade retrofit displacement of $204M by a factor of four to six.[^18] The register survives both readings. The strategic reading raises the option's cost but does not invert the arithmetic against it.

### The bank is getting denser, not flatter

American is doing a second thing at DFW that cuts against a premium reading of the Terminal F floor plate. In December 2025 the airline announced a restructure from 9 to 13 daily connecting banks starting April 2026 — "doubling down on DFW... further strengthens its Flagship hub" in the airline's own framing.[^19] The 9 a.m. peak drops from ~110 to ~52 departures; 40-plus flights move into hours (11 a.m., 1 p.m., 3 p.m., 5 p.m., 9 p.m.) that used to be trough; scheduled block times are being lengthened by an average of 6 minutes across 145 markets to buy connection reliability.[^20]

Most-corroborated read: doubling down on connecting complexity, not shifting toward originating premium. Bank contiguity replaces peak-hour surge; gate concurrency at Terminal F is set by how tightly the 13 banks butt up against each other.

Two caveats. The COO reads the same announcement as a *flatter*, mid-day-loaded operation, which would soften the density case. And the airline's own reported 50% reduction in missed connections is two weeks post-launch, self-reported.[^21] Operational data is more sober: DFW's Q2 2026 departure rate is up only ~1% year over year and remains second-worst among busy US airports; the +6-minute block additions mechanically inflate scheduled on-time performance.[^22] Size Terminal F to the unproven-benefit case, not the press release.

**The connecting-share number is contested and the design has to commit to one.** DFW airport-level reporting places connecting share ~60%; the run prompt asserts a carrier-level ~two-thirds; neither has a primary AA document.[^23] Between 55% and 70% connecting, peak-day originating swings by ~50% — 30,000 vs. 45,000 O&D passenger-trips at American's ~100,000 peak-day.[^24] The v2 draft said "sized to hold either." That is not a design decision. **The register's design basis is 62.5% connecting / 37.5% originating (peak-day O&D ~37,500), with curbside, ticketing hall depth, TSA lane count, and BHS spine sized to that midpoint and structurally oversized to hold 70% originator at an incremental ~$18–25M** — a Category A commitment on Card 6 of the register. That is a decision. The 70% sensitivity is real headroom; the 62.5% midpoint is what the geometry gets sized to.

### History is not neutral about the shape of this building

The industry has run this play — a purpose-built or purpose-adapted hub terminal on a long single-carrier commitment — several times in the deregulation era. In the post-1978 US set at non-trivial hub scale, four cases went badly when the carrier repositioned. Three cases speak affirmatively about a *building* absorbing a *strategy shift*, each with distinct sourcing to be honest about.

Pittsburgh's Midfield Terminal opened October 1, 1992 at roughly $1 billion, with an X-shaped airside optimized for the US Airways connecting bank. US Airways dehubbed in 2004 after a fee dispute the airport could not resolve. Cost per enplaned passenger rose to nearly $15 by 2011, roughly twice the 2002 rate.[^25] Pittsburgh spent fifteen years running a hub airport after the hub had left; stranded midfield debt was not extinguished until roughly 2019; a new $1.75B landside terminal opened in 2025 essentially undoing the 1992 geometry.[^26] Cincinnati went from 22.7 million passengers and 600-plus daily flights in 2005 to under 6 million and under 180 daily by 2013.[^27] St. Louis fell from more than 500 daily American/TWA flights in July 2001 to 207 by 2003.[^28] Cleveland's Concourse D went dark in May 2014 after United's dehub; destinations fell from 61 to 20 and daily departures from about 200 to 72.[^29]

The affirmative cases, ranked by sourcing strength:

**Heathrow Terminal 5** (2008): delivered on time within £4.3B under the T5 Agreement relational contract; a generic multi-satellite hub form that absorbed British Airways' post-merger repositioning, lounge program evolution, and A380 introduction across two decades — then failed operationally at opening with ~42,000 mishandled bags because operational readiness proved undersized.[^30] Sourcing: Global Infrastructure Hub case study on the T5 Agreement; extensive contemporary press. Lesson: generic hub form absorbed strategy pivots; operational readiness discipline is a separate line item and it is real.

**LaGuardia Terminal C** (2022): the four-concourse ~$4B program delivered roughly two years ahead of schedule under single-airline governance — with the honest caveat that ~$3.5B of the $4B was Delta's capital and ~$500M was Port Authority.[^31] Sourcing: Delta Newsroom and MWAA/PANYNJ trade press reporting; program timeline is well-attested. Lesson: single-airline governance delivers when the party accountable holds the capital. The governance test is affirmative; the funding split is a caveat DFW cannot replicate at Terminal F because the airport, not the airline, is holding the pen.

**Detroit McNamara** (2002): SmithGroup-designed linear midfield with satellite concourses for Northwest; Northwest merged into Delta in 2008; Delta kept and still operates the terminal.[^7] Sourcing: SmithGroup design record is primary; cost and post-merger capital history rest on trade-press and enthusiast archives — treat specific dollar figures as directional and the "no material retrofit" claim as the design read available, not audited. Lesson: a hub form that is not carrier-specific in its structural moves (linear midfield, satellite concourses, generic connecting-bank geometry, redundant vertical circulation at the transit node) can absorb a change of tenant intent without material retrofit. It is a survivable-merger case, not a dehub-survival case; the successor kept the hub.

Two honest cautions about the historical file. The dehub cases are survivorship-biased in favor of drama: DFW, ATL, DEN, ORD, IAH, MSP, DTW, SLC, SEA, PHX, and CLT have all held their hub carriers through the entire consolidation wave, and the base rate for a super-hub of DFW's scale being dehubbed inside a 20-year forward window is low. Pittsburgh's 80% single-carrier concentration on a much smaller regional O&D floor is not a like-for-like analogue for DFW's 82.6% American share on a two-city O&D economy of roughly eight million people plus regional output estimated by regional press at *up to* $71.1B (magnitude only).[^32]

The historical warning applies most sharply to the *shape of the building*, not to the *survival of the hub*. Terminal F's shape should be encoded generically enough to survive a strategy shift that history says will arrive — whether or not the carrier does.

### The operative deadline is release-for-fabrication, per module

The run prompt phrases the design-lock horizon as "before the concrete cures." That framing is technically wrong for a modular build. The relevant deadline is closer to "before the module is skinned." For the first six Phase 1 modules, it has already passed.[^1] For the Skylink node, the FIS, and Phase 2 apron modules, the dates are not on the public record and getting them from Innovation Next+ is a 15-day-from-adoption action the register carries.

Modular construction is not a suspension of the physics of retrofit. Managing tolerance stack-up between two large modules fabricated on separate shifts, transported by SPMT, and placed to three-quarter-inch survey is genuinely difficult; when it goes wrong it does not present as a headline, it presents as leaking curtain wall at the module seam, misaligned baggage take-away belts, or life-safety commissioning findings — the exact failure modes that put Berlin Brandenburg nine years and roughly 2.3× budget over.[^33] The ~30% savings figure DFW cites versus conventional construction is credible for on-site duration and reduction of airside impact windows.[^8] What the number is not is a reduction in the design-lock horizon.

A category-based reversibility map, priced honestly, does more useful work than a generic "flexibility" claim.

**Category A — structurally irreversible once modules are set and utilities tied in.** Column grid and floor-to-floor heights. Sterile-corridor geometry into the FIS. Primary baggage tunnel alignment and depth. The apron column-and-jetway grid that determines whether a stand is Code C, Code E, or MARS. Hydrant fuel loop capacity and hydrant pit locations. Primary electrical service and standby generator sizing. 400 Hz and PCA capacity at the stand. The Skylink node, including platform-edge count and vertical circulation redundancy. Some of these are release-for-fabrication-locked, not pour-locked.

**Category B — reversible only at high schedule and airline-consent cost.** Lounge shell subdivisions inside preserved floor plate. Flagship arrival vestibule and dedicated elevator core. CBP FIS booth counts within a preserved hall. TSA checkpoint lane count within preserved queue geometry. BHS in-line EDS matrix within a preserved conveyor spine.

**Category C — cheap to defer, cheap to change later.** Interior finishes and wayfinding. Retail and concession fit-out. Furniture and lounge zoning. Low-voltage overlays: biometrics, digital signage, sensor networks — provided that conduit, riser space, IDF closets, and PoE++ budget were sized generously in Category A.

The strongest, cheapest engineering move is to buy Category C flexibility by paying Category A prices in a small, named set of places. Oversize the risers. Oversize the IDF closets. Oversize the BHS spine. Preserve MARS provisions at a defined minority of stands. That is what "flexible" means when it is priced honestly.

### The 6:15 morning

Every claim above is easier to see if you walk the building on a specific morning. The composite below is built from ledger facts; it is a composite because the airport-side design specifics of the Terminal F Skylink station are not in the public record.[^12]

05:55. Skylink is still running its overnight single-loop pattern — one direction only, roughly 15-minute end-to-end transit, exactly as it does every night from ~22:00 to ~06:00.[^12] The first of American's 13 daily banks is already inbound. Under the restructured schedule the airline expects roughly 52 departures in the 9 a.m. peak — bank contiguity replacing the old surge — which implies a peak-bank population on the order of 5,500 passengers at ~107 passengers per departure per the run's `peak_departure_load_factor`, before any stranded fraction from the previous bank.[^34] Now hold that geometry against the last week of January 2023: winter storm ground stop, four-hour post-stop average delays, more than 1,100 cancellations on the peak day, more than 600 the day after.[^35] Ask which parts of Terminal F's geometry decide whether that morning propagates or dampens.

Four chokepoints do the work. The Skylink node inside Terminal F — platform edges, vertical circulation redundancy, concourse-side queue depth on a morning when the second loop fails to return at 06:00.[^12] Gate concurrency under bank contiguity — how many bank-contiguous gates can turn simultaneously without spillover into taxilane object-free areas defined by FAA AC 150/5300-13B.[^36] MARS stands as the pressure valve that lets one position work either a widebody international arrival or two narrowbody bank turns.[^11] The FIS — CBP's ~100 pax/hr per double booth and 50–75 ft primary queue depth, against a scheduled single-quarter signal in which American is targeting a Q3 2026 DFW capacity expansion of about 6% year over year alongside a roughly 13% LHR reduction, and has added six new summer 2026 international routes (Budapest, Prague, Athens, Milan, Zurich, and expanded Buenos Aires).[^10] [^37]

Every one is a Category A decision locked at release-for-fabrication. If the Skylink station opens without a reserved second platform edge, redundant vertical circulation, and concourse-side queuing sized for an unscheduled single-loop morning, the failure mode is not delayed connections. It is the connecting bank collapsing — the operational metric the airport and the airline both need to defend. If the FIS is sized to a 2025 international peak rather than a properly commissioned 2043 forecast, the airport buys the geometry back with CBP staffing overtime for thirty years.

Premium at Terminal F, on this morning, is what survives the morning: reliable connections, a working Skylink node, an FIS that does not queue back into the sterile corridor. The Flagship-convertible shell rides along as a priced option, not as the headline.

### The register as an executable instrument

The Council's independent research swarm converged on a written reversibility register as the highest-value deliverable — a convergence worth naming and worth discounting. The run prompt itself asked the Council for a "reversibility map," so the vote is not clean corroboration; the register's authority rests on reversibility economics and on the case asymmetry between DTW/LGA/T5 and Pittsburgh/STL/CVG/CLE. The instrument specification follows.

**The register is a DFW Board policy resolution paired with a companion U&L side letter.** The policy resolution is the internal-authority instrument: it commits the CEO's office to a standing monthly review of Category A decisions against the fabrication calendar, requires board-approved disposition of any option before its last-responsible-moment date, and establishes a $10M threshold above which any Category A option requires a signed American cost-recovery memorandum before scope release. The U&L side letter is the counterparty-binding instrument: it obtains American's concurrence on the four register options as a package, priced and dated, and specifies rate treatment. Neither instrument alone is sufficient. The policy resolution without the side letter is airport-side ambition. The side letter without the resolution has no internal governance calendar to enforce it.

Adopting the resolution requires a majority of voting members (6 of 11) at a regularly noticed board meeting; there is no city-council approval requirement for a governance policy of this type.[^38] The Fort Worth minority — 4 of 11 voting seats — is a real politics risk on any mid-program governance instrument perceived as favoring Dallas-appointed leadership or the Dallas-heavy airline-property staff.[^38] The register's protection against that risk is that it is a discipline on cost, not on scope: the seven-field decision-card structure exposes every Category A option to the same test regardless of which side of the airport it sits on.

Two options belong on the register but need a settled contractual answer first. CUPPS-capable gate hardware — an open-standard shell allowing shared physical gate positions with swappable airline software[^39] — is IROPS insurance the operator wants; whether it can be built without triggering an MII objection under the exclusive-use envelope is a legal-architecture question that belongs at the U&L amendment table. A dedicated gate-camera and Assaia-ready mounting envelope with sized PoE++ budget hedges turn-analytics deployment without betting on vendor ROI figures.[^40] Both are register candidates. Neither is a register commitment until the contract question is answered — and the U&L side-letter conversation has its own last-responsible-moment date. The register's fallback if the U&L conversation slips past the Phase 2 tranche: fund CUPPS conduit and camera mounting envelope inside airport-side base building as sunk conduit, defer the active hardware.

The single most important open question is not a design number. It is the contract number: whether the 2025 U&L extension obligates American to any Flagship-tier lounge presence at Terminal F before 2043. If yes, the shell is a design decision on the fabrication calendar. If no, it is a real-options decision at the ratemaking table. The evidence base does not answer the question; the answer determines whether the reversibility discussion is design or contract.[^41]

