## The counter-case, honestly presented

The strongest opposing case runs like this.

American is the party with the most information about its own 2043 network. It has publicly designated Provisions at Terminal F, placed its largest-ever 37,000-sf Admirals Club in Terminal C, and put Flagship Check-In in Terminal D. The airport is being asked to override the tenant's own signal by preloading premium bones the tenant has not requested and has not committed to fund.[^4] Doing so inverts the industry allocation of premium risk, which puts premium fit-out on the carrier's balance sheet — Delta's ~40,000-sf Delta One JFK T4 lounge is a carrier-funded fit-out on Port Authority base building; JetBlue's T5 premium refresh (T5i extension included) is a JetBlue capital story.[^42]

American is simultaneously *expanding* the connecting bank at DFW from 9 to 13 banks — denser peak throughput, tighter minimum connect times, more simultaneous gate demand — which is the opposite physical implication of a premium-shift brief.[^19] The acknowledged premium leader, Delta, has delayed its own Delta One Lounge at its ATL mega-hub to 2028 because industry observers acknowledge Delta has not yet figured out how to execute the concept at its major connecting hubs.[^43] Designing $4B of DFW concrete today around a premium anchor for the industry's follower is asking the airport to bet on an answer even the leader has not published.

American's premium history includes hard retrenchment inside the last five years. In March 2020 the airline closed every Flagship Lounge except JFK — DFW, LAX, MIA, ORD — for roughly 18 months, and briefly converted the JFK Flagship space to standard Admirals Club service.[^44] The balance sheet still constrains the pivot: Q1 2026 total debt of $34.7B (first time below $35B since 2015 but roughly double United's leverage per revenue dollar), a 0.2% net margin in 2025, and a decade-old $3B/$5B profit gap to United and Delta that CEO Robert Isom has publicly framed the premium program as the vehicle to close.[^45] Underwriting $4B of premium-forward capital on the assumption that the follower closes a decade-old profit gap by 2035 is an execution risk American itself has not yet reduced.

Modular's ~30% saving is a repetition dividend, not an infrastructure dividend. Every bespoke MEP tree for a future Flagship kitchen, every double-height structural allowance, every off-standard module for premium features costs the factory its repeat-part cadence. AMR/AA's 2011 Chapter 11 record shows the mechanism is available even when not exercised — 554 non-residential real property leases assumed against 12 rejected in a single September 2013 order, roughly a 2% rejection rate.[^46] The 2% cuts two ways: it tells you AMR kept essentially every hub relationship it had, and it tells you the airport bond record is not one of American reaching for §365 as a routine lever. What it does not tell you is the counterfactual — how much of that "assumed" outcome was priced into renegotiations at the threshold of rejection. The threat is what has value inside a 17-year forward window; the historical usage is what disciplines how the threat is priced.

Every preserved option carries an option premium. Options accumulate into shopping lists no future budget funds. The concession that would make the underlying thesis defensible, on this reading, is much narrower than the underlying thesis implies: preserve utility risers and structural allowance on a small, named number of gate positions, on a fully signed American cost-recovery memorandum, and refuse everything else.

