## Close

The building is not the argument. The register is.

Terminal F opens in phases from 2027 through full opening in 2030, and operates against a lease that expires in 2043 — thirteen to sixteen years of runway depending on where you start the clock. Across that span the carrier's strategy will change more than once. The connecting-bank geometry is already changing. The fleet mix is already changing. The federal-partner staffing model changes annually. The single question the design has to answer is not *what does American want in 2026*. It is: *what shape can the building hold, at low marginal cost bought at fabrication, that survives every plausible turn American takes between now and 2043?*

The tenant's answer is on the record: Terminal F is not the premium anchor. Believe it. Build the connecting building the tenant is asking for. And then, on the airport's own side of the line, buy the small list of geometry options — MARS, FIS envelope, Skylink node, convertible shell, connecting-share oversize — that let the tenant change its mind on the tenant's balance sheet in 2032 without a mid-lease retrofit on the order of the shell displacement itself (call it $200M in the honest low case, that being the foregone gate value the register makes visible).

The register is where that discipline gets signed — a Board policy resolution paired with a U&L side letter, with a $10M threshold, six named cards, priced options, sequenced by quarter. The board should sign it, on the fabrication calendar's clock, before the next module is skinned.

